Koi-DOS LICENSE-MANIFEST
Manifest version: 2
For: KNCL v1.4 (see LICENSE)

This file says which license applies to which material in this
distribution, and which additional permissions that material carries. It
is authoritative for the material it lists. It does not change the terms
of any license; it only says which one applies.

Section 1.5 of KNCL is what gives this file that effect.


TWO KINDS OF LIST

The lists below answer two different questions, and the first version of
this file got into trouble by mixing them.

  A BASE CLASSIFICATION says which license covers a file. Every file has
  exactly one. Where more than one entry could match, precedence decides.

  An ADDITIONAL PERMISSION says what else may be done with a file. A file
  may carry any number of these, or none. They add to the base
  classification and never replace it.

So `include/syscall.h` is KNCL-covered material that is also
redistributable inside a program and may also be reimplemented. Three
statements, all true at once, none of them in conflict. Read as a single
list of exclusive categories they look like a contradiction - which is
what they were until this was written down properly.


PRECEDENCE

Precedence applies to the base classification only, because that is the
question with exactly one answer. Where more than one base entry could
match a file:

  1. An [EXCLUDED] entry wins over everything.
  2. An exact file entry wins over any path or directory entry.
  3. The longest matching path prefix wins.
  4. A directory entry.

The same path must never appear twice within the base classifications. If
it does, that is a bug in this file and not a question to be reasoned
about.

Material that matches no base entry is NOT covered by KNCL through this
manifest. Silence is not permission in either direction: unlisted
material is simply not licensed by this file.

Additional permissions have no precedence, because they do not compete.
An [EXCLUDED] file carries none of them - there is nothing to grant.

Build outputs are covered as the sources they were built from. A kernel
image built from kernel/ is covered; a program built from a third party's
own source is that third party's, whatever it was compiled with.


